@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+# ADR-0015: Consent records are append-only rows in `identity`
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <to be named in review>
+- **Sources:** T3-AC-004, T3-D-009, architecture.md section 4 (`identity` owns consents, emits "consent changed"), TD-08, TD-09, TD-43, AN-900
+
+## Context
+
+Head office must prove each consent of a person (T4 privacy controls). T3-AC-004 asks for a record with the person, the purpose, the text version, the channel (`web`, `advisor`, `import`), the decision and the time, and says that records are never changed or deleted. The `identity` module owns the person (T3-D-009) and the consents (architecture section 4). Other modules, for example `journey` before a send (TD-26 proposal, A4-AC-007), must learn about each change. This adds a table to the data model and an event to the contract between modules.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **One row per decision, append-only.** The current decision is the latest row per person and purpose. Good: the history is the proof; no update path can lose evidence. Bad: reading the current decision needs an ordered read; an index on `(person_id, decided_at, id)` covers it.
+2. **One row per person and purpose, updated in place, plus an audit table.** Good: a simple current read. Bad: two writes that must agree; the proof lives in a second table that can drift from the first.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 1. The table `identity.consent` stores one row per decision. The repository port has only `append` and `findByPerson`; it has no update or delete. After the write, `identity` publishes `identity.consent-changed` with `consentId`, `personId` and `purpose`. The payload carries no decision, because a consent choice is personal data (data conventions); a reacting module reads the decision with `currentConsent`, which also gives the right answer when events arrive late or twice.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The history and the current decision come from one source.
+- A correction is a new record, never an edit. The migration of old consents (out of scope for AN-900) will add rows with channel `import`.
+- Erasure at the end of the retention (`account lifetime + 3 years`) needs a separate, audited job; the application path has no delete. TD-43 decides it.
+- The Drizzle repository must not expose update or delete. A database grant without `UPDATE` and `DELETE` on `identity.consent` should enforce it when the PostgreSQL adapter lands.
+- Tests: `packages/core/src/identity/consent.test.ts` (T3-AC-004).
